#Step 7: Evaluate Results
#Kaplan-Meier Model
#Both Internet Service and Contract variables show significant differences in churn rates based on the log-rank tests.
#The p-values for both variables are highly significant (< 2e-16), indicating their strong association with churn.
#The survival curves for each category within Internet Service and Contract display distinct patterns, suggesting their predictive ability for churn.
#For combine model (All variables), the curve does not drop to 0.5, which confirms that the median survival time isn't reached.

#Cox Model:

#Internet Service:
#Compared to the reference category (DSL), customers with Fiber optic internet service have a significantly higher hazard of churn (HR = 2.00, 95% CI: 1.651-2.427, p < 0.001).
#Customers with no internet service have a significantly lower hazard of churn compared to DSL (HR = 0.26, 95% CI: 0.192-0.339, p < 0.001).
#Internet Service appears to be a strong predictor of churn, with Fiber optic associated with higher churn risk and no internet service associated with lower churn risk.

#Contract:
#Compared to the reference category (Month-to-month), customers with One year contracts have a significantly lower hazard of churn (HR = 0.24, 95% CI: 0.202-0.297, p < 0.001).
#Customers with Two year contracts also have a significantly lower hazard of churn compared to Month-to-month or one year (HR = 0.02, 95% CI: 0.013-0.029, p < 0.001).
#Contract type is a significant predictor of churn, with longer contract durations (One year and Two year) associated with lower churn risk compared to Month-to-month contracts.

#MonthlyCharges & TotalCharges
#MonthlyCharges has HR = 1.04 and TotalCharges has HR = 1, which indicate no significant association with churn risk

#Based on these results, Internet Service and Contract appear to be the most significant variables in predicting churn.

#Step 8: Explain the Model
#Churn prediction models help telecom companies identify customers who are likely to leave (churn) based on factors 
